PRACTICAL NO: 10
TITLE: DESIGN IOT BASED HOME AUTOMATION SYSTEM USING RASPBERRY PI

AIM:
To design and implement an IoT-based home automation system using Raspberry Pi 4 to control multiple electrical appliances via an optocoupler-isolated relay module through a local web dashboard built with Flask.

COMPONENTS REQUIRED:
1. Raspberry Pi 4 Model B (or Pi 3) with Raspberry Pi OS
2. 4-Channel 5V Relay Module (Optocoupler isolated)
3. 4x LEDs with 330 Ohm resistors (or AC lamps for demonstration)
4. Solderless Breadboard
5. Female-to-Female and Male-to-Female Jumper Wires
6. 5V 3A USB-C Power Supply
7. Smartphone, PC, or tablet connected to the same local Wi-Fi network

PIN CONNECTIONS:
Relay VCC connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 2 (5V)
Relay GND connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 6 (GND)
Relay IN1 connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 11 (BCM GPIO 17)
Relay IN2 connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 13 (BCM GPIO 27)
Relay IN3 connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 15 (BCM GPIO 22)
Relay IN4 connects to Raspberry Pi 4 Physical Pin 16 (BCM GPIO 23)

Output Wiring (Per Relay):
Relay COM terminal connects to 3.3V power rail (or AC live line)
Relay NO (Normally Open) terminal connects to LED anode via 330 Ohm resistor (or appliance phase)
LED cathode connects to Ground (or AC neutral return)

THEORY:

1. System Architecture:
An IoT home automation system connects physical appliances to a local or wide-area network for programmatic control. It consists of:
- Perception/Actuation Layer: Relay module acting as an electromechanical actuator to physically open or close high-current paths.
- Controller Layer: Raspberry Pi running a Python Flask application that listens on port 5000 and manipulates GPIO voltage levels.
- Application/Presentation Layer: A browser-rendered dashboard accessible from phones or laptops over local Wi-Fi.

2. Working Principle of Relays:
A relay is an electrically operated switch. Applying an electrical control signal energizes an internal electromagnetic coil, generating a magnetic field that physically pulls a movable armature to toggle between Normally Closed (NC) and Normally Open (NO) contacts.
- Active-LOW Configuration: Most commercial 5V relay modules are active-LOW. Driving a GPIO pin to LOW (0V) completes the circuit through an onboard optocoupler LED, switching the relay ON. Driving the pin HIGH (3.3V) turns the relay OFF.
- Galvanic Isolation: Onboard optocouplers (such as PC817) isolate the low-voltage Raspberry Pi digital logic from the high-voltage load circuit using an internal optical barrier (infrared LED and phototransistor), preventing back EMF and power surges from reaching the processor.

3. Web Server Design (Flask):
Flask is a lightweight Python WSGI web framework. It maps URL routes to Python functions. When a user taps a button on the browser, an HTTP request is dispatched to the Raspberry Pi IP address. The corresponding route changes the target GPIO pin state and updates the web interface status.

